2026-11-17 23:42:39.679 - WARN: The Linux kernel 7.0.5-orbstack-00330-ge3df4e19b0a0-dirty may have a bug that causes random stalls, crashes, and corrupt data on arm64, unless it contains the fix https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/478a1c3abebfc717db0d1281a9cdd7befafee542. As a workaround, QLever doesn't return freed memory to the operating system. On a kernel with the fix, you can disable the workaround by setting the environment variable QLEVER_DISABLE_KERNEL_BUG_WORKAROUND
Options for qlever-index:
  -h [ --help ]                         Produce this help message.
  -v [ --version ]                      Print version information.
  -i [ --index-basename ] arg           The basename of the output files 
                                        (required).
  -f [ --kg-input-file ] arg            The file with the knowledge graph data 
                                        to be parsed from. If omitted, will 
                                        read from stdin.
  -F [ --file-format ] arg              The format of the input file with the 
                                        knowledge graph data. Must be one of 
                                        [nt|ttl|nq]. Can be specified once 
                                        (then all files use that format), or 
                                        once per file, or not at all (in that 
                                        case, the format is deduced from the 
                                        filename suffix if possible).
  -g [ --default-graph ] arg            The graph IRI without angle brackets. 
                                        Write `-` for the default graph. Can be
                                        omitted (then all files use the default
                                        graph), specified once (then all files 
                                        use that graph), or once per file.
  -p [ --parallel-parsing ] arg         Enable or disable the parallel parser 
                                        for all files (if specified once) or 
                                        once per input file. Parallel parsing 
                                        works for all input files using the 
                                        N-Triples or N-Quads format, as well as
                                        for well-behaved Turtle files, where 
                                        all the prefix declarations come in one
                                        block at the beginning and there are no
                                        multiline literals
  -K [ --kg-index-name ] arg            The name of the knowledge graph index 
                                        (default: basename of `kg-input-file`).
  -d [ --text-docs-input-file ] arg     The full text of the text records from 
                                        which to build the text index.
  -w [ --text-words-input-file ] arg    Words of the text records from which to
                                        build the text index.
  -W [ --text-words-from-literals ]     Consider all literals from the internal
                                        vocabulary as text records. Can be 
                                        combined with `text-docs-input-file` 
                                        and `text-words-input-file`
  -T [ --text-index-name ] arg          The name of the text index (default: 
                                        basename of text-words-input-file).
  -A [ --add-text-index ]               Only build the text index. Assumes that
                                        a knowledge graph index with the same 
                                        `index-basename` already exists.
  --bm25-b arg                          Sets the b param in the BM25 scoring 
                                        metric for the fulltext index. This has
                                        to be between (including) 0 and 1.
  --bm25-k arg                          Sets the k param in the BM25 scoring 
                                        metric for the fulltext index.This has 
                                        to be greater than or equal to 0.
  -S [ --set-scoring-metric ] arg       Sets the scoring metric used. Options 
                                        are "explicit" for explicit scores that
                                        are read from the wordsfile, "tf-idf" 
                                        for tf idf and "bm25" for bm25. The 
                                        default is "explicit".
  -s [ --settings-file ] arg            A JSON file, where various settings can
                                        be specified (see the QLever 
                                        documentation).
  --no-patterns                         Disable the precomputation for 
                                        `ql:has-predicate`.
  -o [ --only-pso-and-pos-permutations ] 
                                        Only build the PSO and POS 
                                        permutations. This is faster, but then 
                                        queries with predicate variables are 
                                        not supported
  --add-has-word-triples                Add `ql:has-word` triples for each word
                                        in each literal. This enables keyword 
                                        search in literals via `?literal 
                                        ql:has-word "word"`.
  --has-word-normalization arg          The normalization of the words of the 
                                        `ql:has-word` triples. Literals are 
                                        always split at non-alphanumeric 
                                        characters and the words are 
                                        lowercased; with `lowercase-no-accents`
                                        , all combining marks are also removed 
                                        (e.g. "Café" becomes "cafe"). The 
                                        latter is meant for text in the Latin, 
                                        Greek or Cyrillic script; in other 
                                        scripts it also removes marks that are 
                                        not accents (e.g. the vowel signs of 
                                        Thai). One of lowercase, 
                                        lowercase-no-accents, the default is 
                                        `lowercase`.
  --has-word-restrict-predicates arg    Space-separated list of predicate IRIs 
                                        in angle brackets. If specified, the 
                                        `ql:has-word` triples are only added 
                                        for literals that are the object of a 
                                        triple with one of these predicates. 
                                        Requires `--add-has-word-triples`.
  --vocabulary-type arg                 The vocabulary implementation for 
                                        strings in qlever, can be any of 
                                        in-memory-uncompressed, 
                                        on-disk-uncompressed, 
                                        in-memory-compressed, 
                                        on-disk-compressed, on-disk-compressed-
                                        geo-split
  --geo-point-encoding arg              How geo points are encoded in the IDs 
                                        of the index: `z-order` (the default), 
                                        or `lat-major`, which is how they were 
                                        encoded before 2026-09-26. The encoding
                                        `lat-major` is deprecated, only use it 
                                        if you need to be compatible with 
                                        software that decodes the IDs of an 
                                        index. With it, a spatial prefilter on 
                                        points can only restrict the latitude.
  --encode-as-id arg                    Space-separated list of IRI prefixes 
                                        (without angle brackets). IRIs that 
                                        start with one of these prefixes, 
                                        followed by a sequence of digits, do 
                                        not require a vocabulary entry, but are
                                        directly encoded in the ID. NOTE: When 
                                        using ORDER BY, the order among encoded
                                        IRIs and among non-encoded IRIs is 
                                        correct, but the order between encoded 
                                        and non-encoded IRIs is not
  --iri-as-blank-node-regexes arg       Space-separated list of regexes. An IRI
                                        that is fully matched by one of these 
                                        regexes (via RE2 full match) is not 
                                        stored in the vocabulary, but converted
                                        to a blank node. This saves memory for 
                                        IRIs that only act as internal 
                                        connector nodes (e.g. statement nodes).
                                        The regex is matched against the full 
                                        IRI text including the angle brackets 
                                        and has to cover the entire IRI, so 
                                        each regex must start with `<`; to 
                                        allow an arbitrary suffix, end it with 
                                        `.*`, e.g. the regex 
                                        `<https://example\.org/statement/.*>` 
                                        matches `<https://example.org/statement
                                        /42>`. Only IRIs are affected. NOTE: 
                                        This is an experimental feature. The 
                                        affected IRIs behave as ordinary blank 
                                        nodes, so they are no longer recognized
                                        as those IRIs if used, e.g., in a query
                                        or an update.
  -m [ --stxxl-memory ] arg             The amount of memory in to use for 
                                        sorting during the index build. 
                                        Decrease if the index builder runs out 
                                        of memory.
  -b [ --parser-buffer-size ] arg       The size of the buffer used for parsing
                                        the input files. This must be large 
                                        enough to hold a single input triple. 
                                        Default: 10 MB.
  --index-rows-per-block arg            The number of rows of one block of the 
                                        permutations (and of the other sorted 
                                        lists of the index, like materialized 
                                        views). Index scans always read whole 
                                        blocks, so a smaller value makes 
                                        selective scans read fewer rows, at the
                                        price of more block metadata (which is 
                                        held in RAM) and a slightly larger 
                                        index. The value is stored in the 
                                        index, so that the server uses the same
                                        block size when it writes sorted lists 
                                        (for example, for a materialized view).
                                        Default: 31250.
  -k [ --keep-temporary-files ]         Do not delete temporary files from 
                                        index creation for debugging.
  --materialized-views arg              create materialized views after index 
                                        building. Takes a JSON object mapping 
                                        view names to SELECT queries for 
                                        writing the view, for example: 
                                        {"view1": "SELECT ...", "view2": 
                                        "SELECT ..."}
  --no-resource-usage-log               Disable the resource-usage log. By 
                                        default a TSV log of the RSS, CPU and 
                                        disk I/O of the index build, plus the 
                                        system-wide I/O stall (Linux only), is 
                                        written next to the index files 
                                        (`<index-basename>.index.resource-usage
                                        -log.tsv`).
  --resource-usage-interval-s arg (=1)  The sampling interval of the 
                                        resource-usage log in seconds.
  --log-level arg (=INFO)               Runtime log level: FATAL, ERROR, WARN, 
                                        INFO, DEBUG, TIMING, or TRACE. Default 
                                        is INFO. The compile-time level (INFO) 
                                        applies as an upper bound — messages 
                                        above it are never emitted regardless 
                                        of this setting.
  -j [ --num-threads ] arg              The number of threads used during the 
                                        index build. Must be at least 1. 
                                        Default: the number of hardware threads
                                        of the machine. NOTE: Currently the 
                                        first pass (parsing the input and 
                                        creating the partial vocabularies), the
                                        conversion to global IDs, and the 
                                        shared thread pool that the permutation
                                        writer runs on use this number; the 
                                        other phases use their own parallelism 
                                        (making all phases respect this option 
                                        is work in progress). The memory of the
                                        first pass grows linearly with this 
                                        number, since each thread holds one 
                                        batch of `num-triples-per-batch` 
                                        triples with its partial vocabulary in 
                                        RAM.

